WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.300
Has Disney found its Goku...already?

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.000
Following reports that a Dragon Ball live-action
film is in early development at Disney comes

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.720
the news that the studio may already have
their eye on a candidate for the film's leading

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.290
role: Ludi Lin, who appeared in last year's
Aquaman.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:19.939
This news bite comes courtesy of the rumor-mongers
over at We Got This Covered, though, so take

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:21.500
it with a grain of salt.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.169
Lin is reportedly being considered for Goku,
the good-natured and formerly monkey-tailed

00:00:26.169 --> 00:00:31.990
extraterrestrial warrior in the highly popular
Japanese manga and anime series of the same

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:32.990
name.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:38.290
WGTC is reporting that according to its sources,
Lin is currently on the studio's wish list

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.730
of potential actors to help usher in a new
iteration of the hit anime franchise.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:46.800
Said sources state that at this point, it
is just a wish list, however, and there's

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.140
no word on whether the actor has actually
been contacted by the studio.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.710
Again, it should be pointed out the report
is in no way confirmed, and should be relegated

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:00.650
to the realm of rumor until such time as official
confirmation comes down the pike.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.149
Lin has slowly seen his star rise over the
last several years, starting with his role

00:01:05.149 --> 00:01:10.350
as Zack, the cocky, adrenaline-seeking Black
Ranger in the Lionsgate reboot of the beloved

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.179
and campy '90s Power Rangers franchise.

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:17.350
The film underperformed at the box office,
but Lin's prominence on the project helped

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.759
propel the Chinese-Canadian star into the
spotlight.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:26.140
"This is a bad idea!

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.140
Okay!

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.140
Okay!

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.140
Uh, signals!

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.140
Brakes!

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.140
Brakes!

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.140
I need brakes!

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.140
Manual.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.140
MANUAL!

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.140
Where are the brakes?!"

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.140
Soon after, he appeared as Captain Murk, the
leader of Atlantis' front line army in Aquaman.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.170
He then turned up in a crazy episode of Black
Mirror, "Fighting Vipers", before nabbing

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.770
the role of Liu Kang in New Line Cinema's
upcoming Mortal Kombat reboot, which is slated

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.380
to hit theaters sometime in 2021.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.390
According to WGTC's sources, the new Dragon
Ball flick will have an exclusively Asian

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.209
cast and sport a large budget, a decidedly
different approach to bringing the beloved

00:01:57.209 --> 00:02:01.299
anime to the big screen than 20th Century
Fox's 2009 adaptation.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:05.909
Directed by James Wong and written by Ben
Ramsey, Dragonball Evolution was universally

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:11.370
blasted by critics and fans alike for its
poor writing, weird fight sequences, and white-washed

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.370
cast.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.530
The film had a budget of $35 million, and
made about $55 million at the global box office,

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.709
making it a pretty egregious flop, especially
for such a spectacularly popular property.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:26.680
"I am the hope of the universe!

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.849
I am the answer to all living things that
cry out for peace!"

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:35.790
There are few franchises as popular worldwide
as Dragon Ball; part hero's journey, part

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:41.000
superpowered action story, part tale of friendship,
the cultural influence of the anime has stretched

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.860
far beyond its humble beginnings as a serialized
manga, which was first published in 1984.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.819
Since its initial incarnation, the Dragon
Ball franchise has spawned a number of manga

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:56.050
spin-offs, several big and small screen anime
adaptations, theme park attractions, a series

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:01.329
of soundtracks, companion books, collectible
cards, table top games, video games, and even

00:03:01.329 --> 00:03:02.890
a pop-up cafe.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:06.290
With all of that to its name, it shouldn't
be surprising that it's one of the highest-grossing

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:11.760
media franchises of all time, and one that
major studios definitely want to try to cash

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:12.760
in on.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.530
Following the catastrophe that was the 2009
live action adaptation, though, it seemed

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.800
highly unlikely that Goku and his unforgettable
enemies like Frieza [FREE-zuh] and Janemba

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.530
[juh-NEHM-buh] would ever see the light of
live-adaptation again.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:28.239
Knowing how badly it could go wrong, some
of Dragon Ball's most die-hard fans even started

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:31.769
to believe that no film at all was better
than another bad one.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:36.200
Thanks to the Disney/Fox merger earlier this
year, however, everything has changed.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.940
Disney has become an absolute juggernaut for
producing high-value IP-based franchises;

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.720
the studio that managed to assemble Marvel,
Star Wars, and Pixar under its roof before

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.049
moving on to Fox has been so successful in
converting established IP to box office gold

00:03:52.049 --> 00:03:55.370
that it's got other studios scrambling to
figure out how to to replicate its model with

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:56.879
their own projects.

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:01.430
Fox Studios has seen many of its in-development
movies scrapped since the merger, but that

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.170
doesn't mean that the Mouse House isn't interested
in welcoming Fox's more powerful properties

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.359
into the fold.

00:04:07.359 --> 00:04:12.240
With Dragon Ball's massive cultural clout,
it seems highly likely that Disney will, at

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.910
the very least, consider bringing this beloved
anime universe to life.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.299
Until we get some kind of actual announcement,
though, we'll just have to hope that there's

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:24.770
something to all of the buzz, and for our
money, Lin would make an absolutely amazing

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:25.770
Goku.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.220
We'll keep our eyes peeled for any official
word, and keep you up to speed.

